(function(){
BOOKS['08']={total:12,title:'Human & Settlement Geography',kicker:'Book 08 · Expanded study edition',dek:'Population, demographic change, migration, rural settlements, urban systems, regional inequality and India’s changing human geography.',prev:'07-resources-and-economic-geography.html',next:'09-hazards-and-changing-geography.html',sections:[
{id:'orientation',title:'People, place and scale',html:`<div class="objectives"><h3>Learning outcomes</h3><ul><li>Explain population distribution and demographic change without environmental determinism.</li><li>Use fertility, mortality, age structure, sex ratio and demographic dividend accurately.</li><li>Analyse migration as a household strategy and networked flow.</li><li>Classify rural and urban settlements through site, situation, function and morphology.</li><li>Evaluate Indian regional inequality and urbanisation with dated evidence.</li></ul></div><p>Human geography asks how people organise space and how space shapes opportunity, identity and power. Relief, climate and resources create possibilities and constraints, but technology, institutions, culture and history mediate them. Avoid “people live near rivers because water” as a complete answer. Add land tenure, employment, transport, services, state policy and social networks.</p><div class="glossary"><div><b>Density</b>Population relative to a chosen area.</div><div><b>Demographic transition</b>Shift from high to low birth/death rates.</div><div><b>Dependency ratio</b>Dependent-age population relative to working-age population.</div><div><b>Migration stream</b>Flow between a common origin and destination.</div><div><b>Site</b>Physical ground occupied by a settlement.</div><div><b>Situation</b>Settlement’s relative location and connections.</div></div><div class="concept-box"><b>Three-scale rule:</b> connect household choice, city/region structure and national/global economy. Most human-geography questions fail when answered at only one scale.</div>`},
{id:'distribution',title:'Population distribution, density and carrying capacity',html:`<p>World population clusters in fertile lowlands, temperate and monsoon regions, coasts, river valleys and urban–industrial belts. Sparse areas include extreme cold, arid interiors, high mountains and some dense equatorial forests. Yet physical environment is not destiny: irrigation supports desert cities; resource booms create northern settlements; disease, land policy, colonial history and infrastructure explain many exceptions.</p><p>Arithmetic density divides total population by total area; physiological density relates population to arable land; agricultural density relates farmers to arable land. A national average can conceal crowding in a basin and emptiness in mountains. Density also says little about consumption: a low-density high-consumption population may exert a large distant ecological footprint.</p><p><b>Carrying capacity</b> is not a fixed headcount. It depends on technology, trade, consumption, waste, ecosystem limits and social distribution. Boserup proposed that population pressure can stimulate agricultural intensification, whereas Malthus emphasised population’s tendency to outpace food. Neither is a universal forecast. Food insecurity often reflects access, conflict, poverty, distribution and nutrition rather than global physical shortage alone.</p><p>Population geography therefore uses distributions, flows and composition. Choropleth maps suit rates/density; dot maps show spatial distribution; proportional symbols show totals; cartograms resize space by an attribute. Always check denominator and date.</p><div class="map-task">On a world map shade East Asia, South Asia, Europe and northeastern North America as major concentrations; then mark Sahara–Arabia, Siberia, Greenland, Amazon interior, Himalaya/Tibet and Australian interior as sparse zones. Add one physical and one institutional explanation each.</div>`},
{id:'demography',title:'Demographic change, transition and population structure',html:`<p>Population changes through births, deaths and net migration. Crude birth/death rates use total population denominators and are shaped by age structure; total fertility rate summarises births per woman under current age-specific rates; infant and maternal mortality reflect health and social conditions. Life expectancy is a period measure and does not mean every current resident will live exactly that long.</p><p>The demographic-transition model moves schematically from high births/deaths, through mortality decline and rapid growth, toward low births/deaths and possible natural decrease. Public health, nutrition and sanitation often lower mortality first; education, urbanisation, child survival, women’s agency, contraception and changing costs/benefits later lower fertility. Countries vary and policy/history matter, so stages are an organising model, not an unavoidable timetable.</p><p>A population pyramid displays age–sex composition. A broad base suggests recent high fertility; a bulge records a cohort moving upward; a narrow top indicates fewer older people or mortality. A <b>demographic dividend</b> is potential growth when the working-age share rises—not an automatic bonus. Health, education, women’s employment, job creation, mobility and savings institutions convert potential into productivity. Ageing later raises care, pension and accessible-city needs but also creates a “silver economy.”</p><p>Sex ratio is shaped by sex at birth, differential mortality and migration. National averages conceal age and regional differences. Gender inequality should be analysed through survival, health, education, work, asset ownership, safety and voice, not one ratio alone.</p><div class="diagram"><span class="diagram-title">Dividend conversion chain</span><div class="diagram-flow"><span>fertility decline</span><i>→</i><span>working-age share rises</span><i>+</i><span>health + education + jobs + gender inclusion</span><i>→</i><span>higher productivity and savings</span><i>→</i><span>prepare for ageing</span></div></div>`},
{id:'migration',title:'Migration, mobility and translocal livelihoods',html:`<p>Migration may be internal/international, rural–urban, urban–urban, seasonal, circular, temporary or permanent. Distance and administrative boundaries matter, but so do duration and intent. Commuting is mobility rather than a residential change; circular migrants maintain repeated links between origin and destination.</p><p>Push–pull lists are a starting point: agrarian stress, disasters or conflict may push; jobs, education, marriage or services pull. But households actively diversify risk, networks reduce information and housing cost, recruiters shape routes, and policy controls access. A drought rarely “causes migration” alone; assets, debt, social identity and destination networks decide who can move and where.</p><p>Migration can raise income and remittances, transfer skills, change gender roles and support origin households. Costs include precarious work, family separation, weak social protection, unsafe housing, discrimination and brain drain/care drain. Selectivity matters: migrants are not a random sample of origin population. Women’s migration may be under-read when surveys classify movement mainly by marriage while ignoring work.</p><p>Urban destinations depend on migrants but often link welfare to domicile or formal employment. Portable social protection, rental housing, worker registration, safe transport, childcare and accessible grievance systems reduce vulnerability. Origin policy should expand opportunity without attempting to prevent voluntary mobility.</p><table><tr><th>Lens</th><th>Question</th><th>Evidence</th></tr><tr><td>Household</td><td>Risk diversification or distress?</td><td>Assets, remittance use, gender, season</td></tr><tr><td>Network</td><td>How is the corridor reproduced?</td><td>Recruitment, kin links, transport</td></tr><tr><td>Destination</td><td>Who provides labour and who receives services?</td><td>Sector, housing, portability, rights</td></tr></table><div class="map-task">Draw five Indian migration corridors as arrows only after checking a dated official dataset. Annotate dominant stream, sector, duration and one vulnerability; avoid presenting 2011 counts as current 2026 totals.</div>`},
{id:'rural',title:'Rural settlements and agrarian transformation',html:`<p>Rural settlement form reflects water, relief, soils, land tenure, security, caste/community relations, roads and farm system. <b>Nucleated</b> villages cluster dwellings; <b>dispersed</b> settlements spread farms/homesteads; linear forms follow roads, levees or valleys; circular or rectangular patterns may reflect defence, planned layout or field geometry. Pattern names describe form but do not explain it.</p><p>Site may be a dry point above floods, spring line, valley terrace, river levee or route junction. Situation links the village to markets, towns, schools and transport. In plains, compact settlements may share water and institutions; in mountain or plantation regions, resource dispersion can favour scattered habitation. Tribal hamlets and seasonal pastoral settlements require their own cultural–ecological logic.</p><p>Rural transformation blurs a simple rural/urban divide. Mechanisation, non-farm work, commuting, remittances, digital services and expanded roads produce <b>rurban</b> spaces. Census towns can have urban demographic/economic characteristics without statutory municipal governance, creating infrastructure gaps. Peri-urban villages face land conversion, speculative prices and changing livelihoods.</p><p>Village planning should map habitation, commons, drainage, water source, farms, hazard zones and service access. Land consolidation or road building can improve productivity while removing wetlands or fragmenting grazing routes. Local institutions must include tenant farmers, landless workers and women, not treat “community” as homogeneous.</p><div class="concept-box"><b>Settlement answer:</b> identify form → physical site → historical/social cause → livelihood link → connectivity → service/hazard issue.</div>`},
{id:'urbanisation',title:'Urbanisation, city systems and morphology',html:`<p>Urbanisation is growth in the share and role of urban population, produced by natural increase, net migration and reclassification/expansion of settlements. It differs from urban population growth. Definitions vary by country, making international comparison sensitive to thresholds.</p><p>Cities form systems. A <b>primate city</b> is disproportionately larger and more influential than others; the rank-size rule describes a more graduated hierarchy under ideal conditions. Central-place theory explains service centres and market areas but assumes a uniform surface; transport corridors, government, history and digital services distort hexagonal patterns. Metropolitan regions combine a core, suburbs, satellite towns and peri-urban zones through daily flows.</p><p>Urban morphology includes central business districts, industrial corridors, residential zones, informal settlements and multiple nuclei. Burgess’s concentric model, Hoyt’s sectors and Harris–Ullman’s multiple nuclei are heuristic patterns, not templates to force on Indian cities. Colonial cores, planned sectors, urban villages, ring roads and transit corridors create hybrid forms.</p><p>Agglomeration improves matching, sharing and learning but produces congestion, high land prices, pollution and infrastructure pressure when governance lags. Informality is not merely disorder: it supplies housing, transport, recycling and employment where formal systems exclude, though insecurity and hazards remain. Upgrading with tenure options, services and livelihoods can be superior to distant relocation.</p><div class="diagram"><span class="diagram-title">Metropolitan expansion</span><div class="diagram-flow"><span>core jobs/services</span><i>→</i><span>land cost + congestion</span><i>→</i><span>suburban/peri-urban growth</span><i>→</i><span>commuting and new nodes</span><i>→</i><span>need metropolitan-scale transport, water and land governance</span></div></div>`},
{id:'urban-services',title:'Housing, mobility, water and urban ecological systems',html:`<p>Housing shortage is not only a unit count. Affordable housing must connect secure tenure, sufficient floor space, water/sanitation, schools, work and transport. Peripheral mass housing can be “affordable” in rent but costly in time and lost livelihoods. Rental, dormitory, incremental and in-situ upgrading options serve different households.</p><p>Mobility planning should move people, not maximise vehicle speed. Compact mixed land use and mass transit can shorten trips; walking/cycling need safe continuous networks; buses offer flexible capacity; rail/metro supports high-density corridors but requires feeder integration. Road expansion can induce additional traffic. Transit-oriented development succeeds only if lower-income residents are not displaced from stations.</p><p>Urban water comes from distant basins and local aquifers, then returns as sewage or polluted runoff. Leakage, unequal supply, groundwater extraction and wetland encroachment combine with extreme rain. A water-sensitive city protects catchments and floodplains, separates or manages drainage, harvests rain where suitable, reuses treated wastewater, meters fairly and plans demand.</p><p>Solid waste is a material-flow problem. Segregation at source enables composting and recycling; mixed waste exposes workers and reduces recovery. Informal waste pickers provide a public service and should be integrated safely. Urban green/blue infrastructure cools neighbourhoods, absorbs some stormwater and supports biodiversity, but location and maintenance decide benefits.</p><div class="concept-box"><b>Equity check:</b> map who receives a service, who pays, who travels farthest, who lives beside hazards and who is displaced by the solution.</div>`},
{id:'region',title:'Regions, inequality and place-based policy',html:`<p>A region can be formal (uniform characteristic), functional (flows around a node) or perceptual (shared identity). Administrative borders rarely match labour markets, river basins or ecological zones. Planning must choose boundaries appropriate to the problem.</p><p>Core–periphery patterns arise when capital, skills and decision-making cluster in connected cores while peripheral regions supply labour/resources. Cumulative causation can reinforce advantage; spread effects such as demand, remittances and technology may diffuse it, while backwash effects drain talent and capital. Regional inequality is multidimensional: income, health, education, infrastructure, gender, environment and political voice can diverge.</p><p>Growth poles attempt to stimulate linked activity around leading industries/nodes. Corridors and special zones can create employment but may remain enclaves without local supplier, skill and service links. Fiscal transfers equalise public capacity, while place-based industrial, agricultural or tourism strategies address specific potential. Universal public services remain essential because not every place can host a high-growth cluster.</p><p>Border, hill, island, tribal, drought-prone and conflict-affected regions face different accessibility and institutional costs. Policies should avoid using one “backwardness” label. Strengthen local data and governments, connectivity appropriate to ecology, health/education, value addition, mobility choices and rights. Migration is one adaptation; balanced development means genuine options, not preventing people from leaving.</p><div class="map-task">Create a two-variable India map: choose one opportunity indicator and one deprivation indicator from comparable dated sources. Identify four region types instead of a simplistic developed/backward split.</div>`},
{id:'india',title:'India’s demographic and settlement geography',html:`<p>India’s population is concentrated in the Indo-Gangetic plains, coastal/deltaic belts and urban–industrial regions, with lower densities in high Himalaya, arid desert, forests and islands. State and district differences reflect fertility transition, mortality, migration, urban networks and economic history. Do not quote 2011 values as present-day facts; distinguish <b>last completed census</b>, projections, surveys and administrative data.</p><p>India is at different demographic stages simultaneously. Some states have older age structures and labour shortages; others have younger cohorts and urgent education/job needs. Interstate migration connects these regions. A national demographic-dividend claim is incomplete unless disaggregated by state, gender, education and job structure.</p><p>Urban growth extends beyond statutory municipal boundaries into census towns and peri-urban villages. Large metros dominate finance and specialised services; second-tier cities can distribute growth if they receive capacity and connectivity. Small towns link villages to schools, hospitals, markets and administration but often lack staff and finance.</p><p>The official urban/rural classification and municipal boundaries shape funding and statistics. Metropolitan problems—air sheds, aquifers, waste routes, commuting and flood basins—cross jurisdictions. Strong ward/local data, empowered urban bodies and inter-municipal coordination matter as much as flagship infrastructure.</p><p>As of August 2026, official Census materials identify 2011 as the last completed decennial population census while preparations and first-phase operations for Census 2027 are underway. For the exam, state the reference year beside every census-derived claim and replace this note when new enumeration results are released.</p><div class="misconceptions"><b>Data hygiene:</b> projections estimate a population under assumptions; surveys sample selected indicators; a census aims at complete enumeration. They are not interchangeable.</div>`},
{id:'prelims',title:'Prelims laboratory',html:`<div class="practice-set"><div class="practice-q"><p>Arithmetic and physiological population density always produce the same regional ranking.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>False.</b> Physiological density uses arable land, revealing different pressure.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>Carrying capacity is a permanently fixed number determined only by rainfall.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>False.</b> Technology, trade, consumption, institutions and ecological limits alter it.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>Population change equals births minus deaths plus net migration.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>Correct.</b> At national scale migration may be small; at city/state scale it can dominate.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>A demographic dividend follows automatically whenever working-age share rises.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>False.</b> Jobs, skills, health and inclusion are required.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>A broad-based population pyramid usually suggests high recent fertility.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>Correct,</b> subject to data quality and migration effects.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>Migration is always a permanent change from rural to urban residence.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>False.</b> It includes urban–urban, rural–rural, seasonal, circular and international flows.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>Site means a settlement’s connections to other places.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>False.</b> That is situation; site is the ground/location occupied.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>Urbanisation and growth of urban population are identical.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>False.</b> Urbanisation refers especially to rising urban share/role.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>Central-place theory assumes a uniform surface and purchasing power in its basic form.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>Correct.</b> Real landscapes depart sharply.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>Informal settlements are occupied only by unemployed people.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>False.</b> Residents often supply essential formal and informal labour but lack affordable formal housing.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>A census town and a statutory town are defined through identical governance criteria.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>False.</b> Statutory status follows notified urban local government; census towns meet demographic/economic criteria.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>India’s 2011 Census tables should be labelled as current 2026 enumeration.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>False.</b> Always retain the 2011 reference year and distinguish later projections/surveys.</p></details></div></div>`},
{id:'mains',title:'Mains workshop',html:`<h3>150 words · Why is demographic dividend a regional rather than merely national opportunity?</h3><div class="exam-box"><b>Structure:</b> define age-structure window → states occupy different transition stages → migration links young and ageing regions → education/job/gender gaps vary → state-specific skills, care systems and portable welfare → national coordination.</div><h3>250 words · Urban flooding in India is a governance and land-use problem as much as a rainfall problem. Discuss.</h3><p><b>Model approach:</b> Extreme rainfall supplies the hazard, but urban form determines runoff and exposure. Impervious surfaces, filled wetlands, construction across drains and floodplains, undersized or blocked systems and lost local storage shorten lag and raise peak flow. Multiple agencies divide responsibility for land, drains, roads, lakes and sewage; metropolitan watersheds cross municipal borders. Low-income households are often exposed on hazardous land while receiving the weakest services.</p><p>Response begins with basin-scale elevation, drainage and flood maps integrated into enforceable land use. Protect conveyance corridors and wetlands; restore water bodies as connected systems rather than beautified isolated ponds; redesign roads and public space for temporary storage; separate sewage and stormwater where feasible; maintain drains before monsoon; use forecast-based closure/evacuation; and disclose neighbourhood risk. Building controls, insurance and resilient critical infrastructure must complement blue–green systems. Conclude that climate change raises design stress, but routine governance decides whether intense rain becomes repeated disaster.</p><h3>Further practice</h3><ol><li>Explain population concentration through physical and historical factors.</li><li>Analyse circular migration as a household risk strategy.</li><li>How is peri-urbanisation changing rural land and governance?</li><li>Can second-tier cities support more balanced regional development?</li></ol><div class="map-task">Practise world population concentration, an India density/urban hierarchy sketch, one migration corridor map and a metropolitan core–periphery diagram.</div>`},
{id:'sources',title:'Sources and final recall',html:`<ul class="source-list"><li><a href="https://censusindia.gov.in/census.website/en">Census of India</a> — official census status, tables, maps and Census 2027 notices.</li><li><a href="https://censusindia.gov.in/census.website/en/data/tables">Census tables</a> — population, migration, settlement and social data with reference years.</li><li><a href="https://censusindia.gov.in/census.website/en/demograghy">ORGI Demography Division</a> — projections and demographic analysis.</li><li><a href="https://mohua.gov.in/">Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs</a> — urban programmes and reports.</li><li><a href="https://mospi.gov.in/">Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation</a> — surveys and statistical publications.</li><li><a href="https://www.upsc.gov.in/examinations/previous-question-papers">UPSC previous papers</a>.</li></ul><div class="concept-box"><b>Final retrieval:</b> Explain distribution, transition, migration, rural form, urban system and inequality with one model, one India example and one policy limitation each.</div><p class="source-note">Concept edition updated August 2026. Officially, 2011 remains the last completed decennial census and Census 2027 operations are underway; update after release of new results.</p>`}
],recall:['Compare three density measures.','Explain carrying capacity without determinism.','Draw and read three population pyramids.','Turn demographic window into a policy chain.','Classify migration by boundary, duration and motivation.','Explain one migration corridor at three scales.','Distinguish site, situation, form and function.','Compare nucleated, dispersed and linear settlements.','Distinguish urban growth from urbanisation.','Apply three city morphology models critically.','Explain metropolitan services as ecological flows.','Label every demographic statistic with source and year.']};

BOOKS['09']={total:12,title:'Hazards & Changing Geography',kicker:'Book 09 · Expanded study edition',dek:'Multi-hazard risk, vulnerability, disaster governance and the changing location of glaciers, rivers, coasts, water bodies, vegetation and species.',prev:'08-human-and-settlement-geography.html',next:'10-map-and-atlas-workbook.html',sections:[
{id:'orientation',title:'From hazard catalogues to risk systems',html:`<div class="objectives"><h3>Learning outcomes</h3><ul><li>Distinguish hazard, exposure, vulnerability, capacity, risk and disaster.</li><li>Explain geological, hydro-meteorological and climatic hazard processes.</li><li>Analyse cascading and compound risk across infrastructure and ecosystems.</li><li>Use prevention, mitigation, preparedness, response and recovery correctly.</li><li>Answer GS-I questions on changes in critical geographical features.</li></ul></div><p>A hazard is a potentially damaging event or process; exposure is what lies in harm’s way; vulnerability is susceptibility; capacity reduces harm; disaster is severe disruption exceeding coping ability. A simple heuristic is <b>risk ≈ hazard × exposure × vulnerability, moderated by capacity</b>. It is not a literal universal equation, but it prevents the exam error of treating an earthquake or cyclone as a disaster by itself.</p><div class="glossary"><div><b>Return period</b>Inverse of annual exceedance probability under assumptions.</div><div><b>Resilience</b>Capacity to resist, absorb, adapt and recover while retaining function.</div><div><b>Cascade</b>One failure triggers further hazards or system failures.</div><div><b>Compound event</b>Multiple drivers/hazards combine.</div><div><b>Maladaptation</b>Action that increases future or transferred risk.</div><div><b>Build back better</b>Recovery that reduces future risk and inequity.</div></div><div class="concept-box"><b>Risk sentence:</b> “The physical trigger was __; losses concentrated because exposure __ and vulnerability __; the priority is therefore __.”</div>`},
{id:'risk-cycle',title:'Risk assessment and the disaster-management cycle',html:`<p>Risk assessment begins with hazard characterisation—where, how often, how intense and under what scenarios. Exposure mapping locates people, buildings, lifelines, ecosystems and economic activity. Vulnerability assessment examines construction, poverty, age/disability, livelihood sensitivity, environmental degradation and institutional access. Capacity includes knowledge, networks, savings, services, shelters and response systems.</p><p>Maps must express uncertainty. A hazard zonation map is not a prediction of exact time; past frequency may shift under climate/land-use change; coarse national maps cannot decide plot-level safety. Participatory mapping identifies local drains, evacuation constraints and vulnerable groups missed by remote data.</p><p>The cycle includes prevention/avoidance, mitigation, preparedness, response, recovery and reconstruction, but work overlaps. Land-use zoning and safe construction reduce risk before events; monitoring, warning, drills and supplies prepare; incident command, search, health and relief respond; livelihood recovery, reconstruction and psychosocial support follow. Recovery is a window to improve standards but may dispossess residents if “safer” rebuilding ignores tenure and livelihood.</p><p>Residual risk remains after protection. A levee or seawall may reduce frequent losses while encouraging exposure behind it; failure then becomes catastrophic. Combine structural and non-structural measures, maintenance, evacuation and transparent design limits. Insurance transfers financial loss but does not prevent death or ecosystem damage.</p><div class="diagram"><span class="diagram-title">Risk-governance loop</span><div class="diagram-flow"><span>hazard + exposure + vulnerability</span><i>→</i><span>risk assessment</span><i>→</i><span>avoid / reduce / prepare / transfer</span><i>→</i><span>event response</span><i>→</i><span>recovery and learning</span><i>→</i><span>update risk map</span></div></div>`},
{id:'geological',title:'Earthquakes, volcanoes and tsunamis',html:`<p>Earthquake risk concentrates near active plate boundaries and faults, but intraplate earthquakes also occur. Magnitude measures event size; intensity varies by location. Damage reflects shaking frequency/duration, depth, distance, soft sediment amplification, liquefaction, slope failure, fire and construction. Short buildings and tall buildings respond differently to shaking periods, so “stronger equals safer” is insufficient without engineering.</p><p>Prevention of tectonic earthquakes is not possible, but disaster losses are reducible through microzonation, code-compliant design, retrofitting, lifeline redundancy, securing non-structural elements, drills and land-use restrictions on fault rupture/liquefaction or unstable slopes. Early-warning systems detect initial waves after rupture and may provide seconds—not long-range prediction.</p><p>Volcanic hazards include lava, ash, pyroclastic density currents, gases, lahars and slope collapse. Ash can disrupt aviation and roofs far beyond lava zones. Monitoring deformation, gases and seismicity improves eruption forecasts, but exact timing remains uncertain.</p><p>Tsunamis arise when water is rapidly displaced, especially by large shallow undersea earthquakes, but also landslides or volcanism. Deep-ocean detection, seismic networks, models, sirens and evacuation routes form an end-to-end system. Natural signs—strong/long coastal shaking, sudden sea withdrawal or roar—demand immediate movement to high ground without waiting for confirmation.</p><div class="map-task">Map Himalayan seismic belt, Kachchh, peninsular intraplate examples, Andaman–Sunda zone, Barren Island and Indian Ocean tsunami-exposed coasts. Add construction/evacuation implications rather than copying zone numbers alone.</div>`},
{id:'hydromet',title:'Cyclones, floods, droughts and extreme heat',html:`<p>Cyclone risk combines wind, surge, wave, rain, river flood and cascading power/communication failures. Forecast accuracy has improved, but last-mile warning, safe shelter, livelihood evacuation, resilient housing and rapid restoration determine impact. Mangroves and dunes reduce some energy but do not substitute for evacuation or safe structures.</p><p>Flood types include riverine, flash, pluvial/urban, coastal and glacial-lake outburst. Causes differ: basin rainfall and snowmelt; intense local storms; blocked drainage; surge; dam/landslide-lake breach. Measures must match type. Floodplain zoning, wetlands, reservoir coordination, catchment management, raised infrastructure and forecast-based action complement embankments and channels. “Desilting every river” is neither universally feasible nor ecologically neutral.</p><p>Meteorological drought is rainfall deficit; agricultural drought is inadequate soil moisture; hydrological drought is reduced stream/reservoir/groundwater; socio-economic drought describes failure to meet demand. They occur on different lags. Preparedness includes water accounting, crop choice, soil moisture, storage, groundwater rules, fodder, livelihood support and drought-triggered safety nets.</p><p>Heat waves become disasters through high temperature, humidity, warm nights, urban form, occupation, age/health and service failure. Heat action plans combine forecasts, communication, health readiness, work schedules, water/shade and long-term cool/green design. Cooling access must grow without worsening peak power, waste heat and emissions.</p><table><tr><th>Hazard</th><th>Lead indicator</th><th>High-value action</th></tr><tr><td>Cyclone</td><td>Track, wind, rain, surge forecast</td><td>Evacuate people/livestock; resilient lifelines</td></tr><tr><td>River flood</td><td>Rainfall, upstream gauge and model</td><td>Floodplain management + warning</td></tr><tr><td>Drought</td><td>Rain, soil moisture, reservoir/aquifer trend</td><td>Staged demand and livelihood plan</td></tr><tr><td>Heat</td><td>Temperature, humidity and night minima</td><td>Health-triggered action + urban redesign</td></tr></table>`},
{id:'mountains',title:'Landslides, avalanches and mountain hazard cascades',html:`<p>Landslides occur when downslope driving stress exceeds material strength. Susceptibility depends on slope, lithology, structure, weathering, drainage and vegetation; triggers include intense/prolonged rain, earthquakes, snowmelt, toe erosion and excavation. Types—fall, slide, slump, flow—have different speeds and run-out, so one generic retaining-wall solution is unsafe.</p><p>Road cuts can over-steepen slopes, expose weak planes and block natural drainage; dumped spoil enters channels; leaking water raises pore pressure. Hydropower, buildings and quarrying interact cumulatively in a valley. A slide may block a river, impound water and later breach; sediment can aggrade downstream channels. Hazard assessment therefore includes source, path/run-out and deposition zones.</p><p>Mitigation combines avoiding very high-risk zones, drainage, slope regrading/support, vegetation where appropriate, controlled blasting/cutting, debris management, monitoring and rainfall thresholds. Community observation of cracks/springs complements instruments. A landslide inventory records past events; susceptibility maps where events are more likely; hazard adds probability/time; risk adds exposed assets.</p><p>Avalanches require snowpack, slope and trigger. Wind slabs, weak layers, warming or new snowfall affect stability. Zonation, controlled release, snow structures, route closure, forecasting and rescue readiness reduce risk. Glacial valleys also face ice/rock avalanches and lake outburst floods, often in remote cascading systems.</p><div class="diagram"><span class="diagram-title">Mountain cascade</span><div class="diagram-flow"><span>extreme rain / earthquake / ice-rock failure</span><i>→</i><span>slope collapse</span><i>→</i><span>river blockage</span><i>→</i><span>temporary lake</span><i>→</i><span>breach flood + sediment pulse</span><i>→</i><span>downstream infrastructure failure</span></div></div>`},
{id:'industrial',title:'Industrial, technological and biological hazards',html:`<p>Industrial risk arises from hazardous substances, process failure, unsafe storage/transport, fire/explosion and weak maintenance. Spatial planning should separate incompatible uses while recognising existing settlements around old plants. On-site and off-site emergency plans, inventories, sensors, trained responders, hospital preparedness and public right-to-know form a system. Chemical plumes follow wind and terrain; river pollution follows drainage; therefore jurisdictional boundaries are inadequate.</p><p>Critical infrastructure—power, telecom, water, health, transport and digital systems—is interdependent. Flooded substations disable pumps and communication; a heat wave raises electricity demand while lowering some generation efficiency; cyber or grid failure can disrupt hospitals. Resilience uses redundancy, diversity, modularity, backup energy, protected sites, spare parts and tested restoration priorities.</p><p>Biological hazards include epidemics, zoonoses, crop/livestock disease and invasive organisms. Land-use change, wildlife trade, dense mobility and climate can alter contact and vector suitability. One Health connects human, animal and ecosystem surveillance. Geographic tools support hotspot mapping and access planning, but privacy and bias matter.</p><p>Multi-hazard planning avoids maladaptation. A cyclone shelter outside surge zone may still be inaccessible across a flooded route; a sealed heat-resistant building may worsen indoor air without power; chemical storage relocated from a river may enter a landslide zone. Scenario exercises should test compound failures and vulnerable groups.</p><div class="concept-box"><b>Lifeline test:</b> for every hazard, ask what happens if power, water, telecom, road and hospital capacity fail together for 72 hours.</div>`},
{id:'cryosphere',title:'Changing glaciers, ice caps and permafrost',html:`<p>Glaciers gain through snowfall/avalanching and lose through melt, sublimation and calving. <b>Mass balance</b> is annual gain minus loss; retreat of the terminus is a geometric response influenced by flow and can lag. One advancing glacier does not negate regional loss, and one retreating tongue is not by itself a global dataset.</p><p>Warming shifts snowline/equilibrium-line altitude, changes snow-to-rain ratios and alters melt timing. Initially, higher melt may raise warm-season runoff; as ice volume declines, glacier contribution may fall. Basin response varies with glacier fraction, monsoon/westerly precipitation, groundwater and dams. Black carbon and dust lower snow/ice albedo and accelerate melt.</p><p>Glacial lakes can expand behind moraine or ice dams as glaciers retreat. Outburst risk depends on lake growth, dam stability and triggers such as ice/rock avalanche. Inventories identify lakes; field and remote monitoring, controlled drainage where appropriate, land-use controls and downstream warnings manage risk. Not every expanding lake is immediately dangerous.</p><p>Permafrost is ground remaining at or below freezing for at least two consecutive years; it is not permanently snow-covered ground. Thaw destabilises slopes and foundations, alters hydrology and can release greenhouse gases from organic-rich soils. Ice caps and ice sheets affect global sea level, while floating sea-ice melt has little direct sea-level effect but strongly changes albedo and ecosystems.</p><div class="map-task">Mark Hindu Kush–Karakoram–Himalaya, Tibetan Plateau, Greenland, Antarctica, Arctic permafrost and major high-mountain regions. For India, annotate western-disturbance vs monsoon influence.</div>`},
{id:'coasts-rivers',title:'Changing coasts, rivers and water bodies',html:`<p>Coastlines move through waves, tides, currents, sediment supply, storms, sea-level change and land movement. Erosion at one location may represent natural sediment-cell adjustment rather than net regional loss. Groynes and seawalls can protect assets while reflecting waves or starving downdrift beaches. Dams reduce river sediment; sand mining removes channel/coastal material; subsidence from sediment compaction or groundwater extraction raises relative sea level.</p><p>Deltas are especially sensitive because they are low, sediment-dependent and densely occupied. Resilience combines upstream sediment and flow management, wetland/mangrove space, subsidence control, cyclone preparedness, setback/zoning and selective protection. “Plant mangroves everywhere” ignores salinity, tidal elevation and sediment suitability.</p><p>River courses change through meander migration, avulsion, bank erosion, incision, sedimentation and engineering. Channelisation may accelerate water downstream; embankments alter floodplain exchange; dams trap sediment and change temperature/seasonality. Restoration should define a realistic reference and allow room for process, rather than forcing every river into one historical line.</p><p>Water bodies shrink or expand through climate variability, river diversion, groundwater interaction, siltation, extraction, encroachment and dams. Remote-sensing area change alone does not reveal volume, depth, salinity or cause. Urban “lake restoration” fails when sewage inflow and catchment drainage remain untreated.</p><div class="diagram"><span class="diagram-title">Delta vulnerability chain</span><div class="diagram-flow"><span>sediment trapping + extraction subsidence</span><i>+</i><span>sea-level rise and cyclone surge</span><i>+</i><span>wetland conversion</span><i>→</i><span>relative water level and exposure rise</span><i>→</i><span>salinity, erosion and flood risk</span></div></div>`},
{id:'flora-fauna',title:'Changing flora, fauna and landscapes',html:`<p>Species distributions shift when temperature, rainfall, seasonality, disturbance and interactions change. Many move poleward or upslope, but response is constrained by soil, habitat, barriers, competitors and dispersal. At mountain tops or islands, suitable area can shrink with nowhere further to move. Phenology—timing of flowering, migration or breeding—may change at different rates across interacting species.</p><p>Land-use change is often the immediate driver: fragmentation isolates populations, roads increase edges and mortality, altered fire/grazing changes vegetation, and invasive species exploit disturbance. Climate change compounds rather than replaces these pressures. Marine warming, acidification and deoxygenation affect corals, plankton and fish distributions; river warming and altered flow affect freshwater species.</p><p>Range shifts affect livelihoods, crop pests, disease vectors, fisheries and protected-area design. Static reserves remain essential refuges but need elevation/latitude corridors, buffer landscapes and climate refugia. Assisted migration is debated because it may rescue species but introduce ecological risk; decisions require strong evidence.</p><p>Landscape restoration should rebuild connectivity, hydrology, native structure and disturbance regimes. Planting a monoculture may raise canopy area but reduce resilience. Monitoring uses repeated plots, citizen science, acoustic/camera data and remote sensing, with baselines and detection uncertainty.</p><table><tr><th>Observed change</th><th>Possible drivers</th><th>Evidence needed</th></tr><tr><td>Species found higher</td><td>Warming, land use, survey effort</td><td>Repeated comparable range data</td></tr><tr><td>Lake area declines</td><td>Drought, diversion, silt, groundwater</td><td>Climate, inflow/outflow and depth</td></tr><tr><td>Coast retreats</td><td>Storms, sediment deficit, rise/subsidence</td><td>Long shoreline and sediment-cell record</td></tr></table>`},
{id:'india-system',title:'India’s multi-hazard geography and governance',html:`<p>Himalayan and Northeast belts combine earthquakes, landslides, avalanches, glacial lakes, intense rain and river cascades. Indo-Gangetic plains face floods, bank erosion, heat, fog/air pollution and earthquake amplification in some sediments. The plateau and dry interior face drought, heat, forest fire, hard-rock groundwater stress and local floods. Coasts/deltas face cyclone, surge, erosion, salinity and tsunami in relevant zones. Cities superimpose dense exposure and infrastructure interdependence.</p><p>India’s disaster-management architecture spans national, state, district and local authorities, line departments and specialised agencies. Effective management is not the response agency alone: urban development enforces building/land rules; irrigation operates reservoirs; health prepares surveillance; schools and communities practise evacuation; finance enables resilient investment.</p><p>Early warning must be end to end: risk knowledge → monitoring/forecast → authoritative understandable message → last-mile reach → trusted action capability → feedback. A technically accurate forecast fails if fishers cannot receive it, livestock cannot evacuate, shelters exclude disabled people or households fear loss of assets.</p><p>Development choices create future risk. Environmental and social assessment should incorporate climate/multi-hazard scenarios, cumulative impacts and safe failure. Local disaster plans should inventory people needing assistance, shelters, routes, equipment, contacts and lifelines and be exercised, not copied annually.</p><div class="map-task">Create one Indian multi-hazard map with no more than six colours. Overlay population/critical infrastructure mentally and write five compound-risk corridors: Himalayan valley, Brahmaputra plain, arid city, east-coast delta and west-coast metropolis.</div>`},
{id:'prelims',title:'Prelims laboratory',html:`<div class="practice-set"><div class="practice-q"><p>A hazard becomes a disaster only when its physical magnitude crosses a universal threshold.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>False.</b> Exposure, vulnerability and capacity determine disruption.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>A hazard map automatically includes vulnerability and exposed assets.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>False.</b> Those additions create risk assessment.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>An earthquake early-warning alert is the same as predicting an earthquake days beforehand.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>False.</b> It detects rupture already begun and may offer seconds before stronger waves.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>Tsunami is an astronomical tide amplified by an earthquake.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>False.</b> It is a separate long wave from rapid water displacement.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>Meteorological, agricultural and hydrological drought can begin and end at different times.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>Correct.</b> Soil, reservoirs and aquifers respond with different lags.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>Heat risk can remain high at night even when daytime maximum falls.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>Correct.</b> Warm nights prevent physiological recovery.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>A landslide inventory, susceptibility map and risk map are interchangeable.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>False.</b> Past events, likelihood and exposed consequences are distinct layers.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>Floating sea-ice melt directly raises sea level as much as equal land-ice melt.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>False.</b> Floating ice already displaces water; land ice adds water to the ocean.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>Every expanding glacial lake has the same outburst probability.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>False.</b> Dam, geometry, triggers and downstream path differ.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>Hard coastal protection can transfer erosion to a downdrift beach.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>Correct.</b> Structures alter sediment movement.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>A shrinking lake seen in one satellite image pair proves climate change as the sole cause.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>False.</b> Diversion, extraction, sediment and natural variability require investigation.</p></details></div><div class="practice-q"><p>Resilience means restoring every system exactly to its pre-disaster state.</p><details class="answer"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><b>False.</b> Adaptation or transformation may be needed; restoring prior vulnerability is not resilient.</p></details></div></div>`},
{id:'mains',title:'Mains workshop',html:`<h3>150 words · Distinguish a hazard map from a risk map.</h3><div class="exam-box"><b>Structure:</b> hazard = location/intensity/probability → exposure = people/assets → vulnerability/capacity → risk = likely consequence → example of earthquake microzone + building stock → uncertainty and update.</div><h3>250 words · “The Himalaya should be planned as a cascade of connected hazards.” Explain.</h3><p><b>Model approach:</b> Active tectonics and steep fractured relief create earthquake and landslide susceptibility. Intense rain, snowmelt or ice/rock failure can trigger slope collapse; debris may dam rivers; impounded water can breach into a flash flood carrying sediment that blocks roads, hydropower and communication. Glacier retreat may enlarge lakes, while road cuts, spoil disposal and settlements raise exposure. A project safe against one local slope can still fail through an upstream cascade.</p><p>Risk reduction therefore needs catchment/valley-scale inventories of source, run-out and inundation zones; cumulative assessment of roads and hydropower; protected drainage and scientific debris disposal; rainfall, movement and lake monitoring; redundant communication and evacuation routes; land-use controls around unstable slopes and flood paths; resilient bridges/lifelines; and local drills. Where uncertainty and consequence are extreme, avoiding construction is safer than engineering confidence. Conclude that connected-process planning replaces fragmented project clearances with a living multi-hazard system.</p><h3>Further practice</h3><ol><li>Why does improved cyclone forecasting not eliminate cyclone disaster?</li><li>Discuss urban heat as a compound climatic and social risk.</li><li>How do human interventions alter the geography of deltas and coasts?</li><li>Examine evidence required before attributing a changing water body or species range.</li></ol><div class="map-task">Practise a multi-hazard India map plus cross-sections for liquefaction, landslide cascade, cyclone surge, flood hydrograph, glacial lake and delta subsidence.</div>`},
{id:'sources',title:'Sources and final recall',html:`<ul class="source-list"><li><a href="https://ndma.gov.in/">National Disaster Management Authority</a> — official guidelines and plans.</li><li><a href="https://nidm.gov.in/documentations.asp">National Institute of Disaster Management documentation</a> — current Indian case material and training references.</li><li><a href="https://bhusanket.gsi.gov.in/">GSI Bhusanket</a> — landslide inventory, susceptibility and warning resources.</li><li><a href="https://mausam.imd.gov.in/">IMD</a> — weather warnings and climate services.</li><li><a href="https://incois.gov.in/">INCOIS</a> — tsunami, surge, wave and ocean advisories.</li><li><a href="https://www.nrsc.gov.in/nrscnew/Dataproducts_Thematic_overview.php">NRSC thematic maps</a> — floods, landslides, glacial lakes, water bodies and coasts.</li></ul><div class="concept-box"><b>Final retrieval:</b> For earthquake, cyclone, flood, drought, heat, landslide and coastal change, state process, exposed region, vulnerability, warning limit and one structural plus one non-structural measure.</div><p class="source-note">Concept edition updated August 2026. Never reuse old event counts or zone maps without date and source; official alerts override study material.</p>`}
],recall:['Define hazard, exposure, vulnerability, capacity and disaster.','Turn a hazard map into a risk assessment.','Distinguish prediction, forecast and early warning.','Build one earthquake loss chain.','Compare five flood types.','Separate four drought concepts.','Explain compound heat risk.','Classify landslide products and maps.','Distinguish glacier mass balance from terminus change.','Explain relative sea-level change.','Evaluate evidence for changing geographical features.','Draw five Indian compound-risk corridors.']};
})();
